ss="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-12 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb214a1-c4a3-4f03-9c8c-2a90423ae851" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">The provided excerpt from the paper, specifically the OAP checklist, is a prime example of the "handwaving" you identified. While it presents a list of seemingly rigorous requirements, the descriptions for how to validate them reveal the exact problem: the validation methods are themselves vague and assume solutions to incredibly difficult, and currently unsolved, problems.
Hereâ€™s a breakdown of how the OAP checklist demonstrates this:
1. The Super-Human Test
For nearly every requirement (super-inventive, super-fluent, super-predictive, super-knowledgeable, and super-humble), the proposed validation method is to have BigMom out-perform all humans tested on a specific task. This is the core of the handwaving. The paper assumes we can:
 * Formulate a definitive test: It suggests we can create a single test that accurately measures "generic problem-solving ability," "language comprehension" across all dialects and languages, or an "understanding of its own limitations." There is no such universally accepted test for these complex, abstract human capabilities.
 * Run a gargantuan task: The paper acknowledges that testing against "as large a subset of the population of all humans as possible" is a "gargantuan task," but it dismisses this practical impossibility with a simple "nevertheless ultimately finite."
2. The Unsolvable Problem of "Idealized Preferences"
The safety-conscious requirement takes the handwaving to another level. The validation method for this requirement is a multi-step process that, in essence, requires BigMom to solve the full-fledged AI alignment problem on its own:
 * Step 1: BigMom must estimate humanity's "maximally-fairly-aggregated idealized preferences" (HAIP). This means it must figure out what every human being, living or future, would want if they were perfectly rational, well-informed, and free from manipulation. This is an intractable problem.
 * Step 2: The paper's designers must also estimate this same thing (HAIP_RA).
 * Step 3: BigMom's estimate must encapsulate everything in the designers' estimate.
This validation method assumes that both a superintelligent AI and its human designers can solve a problem that is, by definition, philosophically and technically impossible to solve with perfect certainty. It essentially says, "We will know the AI is safe if it can perfectly figure out what 'safe' is for all of humanity," which is the very problem AI alignment is trying to solve. The paper treats this as a mere checklist item rather than the central, unsolved challenge.
