64 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="5f947e7d-dbf0-49dc-aac0-4d638dfc3592" data-testid="conversation-turn-4" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="5f947e7d-dbf0-49dc-aac0-4d638dfc3592" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="344">Youâ€™re exactly right â€” the OAP checklist <em data-start="41" data-end="48">looks</em> like a rigorous validation framework, but under scrutiny it reveals precisely the â€œhandwavingâ€ problem: the hardest open questions in AI safety and cognitive science are treated as though they were merely engineering details. Let me structure this critique in a way that lays bare the problem.</p>
<hr data-start="346" data-end="349">
<h2 data-start="351" data-end="407">1. The Super-Human Test: An Illusion of Objectivity</h2>
<p data-start="409" data-end="659">For the first five OAP requirements (super-inventive, super-fluent, super-predictive, super-knowledgeable, super-humble), the validation principle is essentially the same: <strong data-start="581" data-end="657">BigMom must outperform all humans on some definitive test of capability.</strong></p>
<p data-start="661" data-end="704">The handwaving here shows up in two ways:</p>
<ul data-start="706" data-end="2029">
<li data-start="706" data-end="1494">
<p data-start="708" data-end="739"><strong data-start="708" data-end="737">No universal tests exist.</strong></p>
<ul data-start="742" data-end="1494">
<li data-start="742" data-end="1018">
<p data-start="744" data-end="1018"><em data-start="744" data-end="770">Problem-solving ability:</em> There is no single task or suite of tasks that can exhaustively capture â€œgeneric problem-solving.â€ Any benchmark (IQ tests, Ravenâ€™s matrices, SAT problems, etc.) measures at best a narrow proxy, and humans vary dramatically depending on context.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1021" data-end="1257">
<p data-start="1023" data-end="1257"><em data-start="1023" data-end="1048">Language comprehension:</em> Defining a measure for â€œany meaningful fragment of any known languageâ€ that captures semantic distance Î”(MCa, MCb) is itself an unsolved research program spanning linguistics, philosophy of meaning, and AI.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1260" data-end="1494">
<p data-start="1262" data-end="1494"><em data-start="1262" data-end="1300">Understanding oneâ€™s own limitations:</em> Self-calibration in humans is assessed via crude psychological experiments (e.g., Lichtenstein, Dunningâ€“Kruger), but scaling that to an AI systemâ€™s entire epistemic state is not well-defined.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1496" data-end="2029">
<p data-start="1498" data-end="2029"><strong data-start="1498" data-end="1536">The scale is dismissed as trivial.</strong><br data-start="1536" data-end="1539">
The paper itself admits that testing BigMom against â€œas large a subset of the population of all humans as possibleâ€ is a â€œgargantuan taskâ€ â€” and then simply asserts that it is â€œultimately finite,â€ as though that sidesteps the feasibility barrier. In practice, this is not just gargantuan but functionally impossible: population-scale testing across all human domains of knowledge, languages, and predictive contexts is a research agenda larger than the entire project of modern science.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2031" data-end="2234">This makes the checklist a tautology: BigMom is validated if it demonstrates superhuman ability, and it demonstrates superhuman ability if it passes tests that no one knows how to define or administer.</p>
<hr data-start="2236" data-end="2239">
<h2 data-start="2241" data-end="2300">2. The Safety-Conscious Requirement: Alignment by Fiat</h2>
<p data-start="2302" data-end="2446">The most glaring example is the <strong data-start="2334" data-end="2354">safety-conscious</strong> requirement, which collapses the full AI alignment problem into a single validation step.</p>
<p data-start="2448" data-end="2478">The validation procedure is:</p>
<ol data-start="2479" data-end="2744">
<li data-start="2479" data-end="2603">
<p data-start="2482" data-end="2603">BigMom estimates <strong data-start="2499" data-end="2507">HAIP</strong> (the maximally-fairly-aggregated idealized preferences of humanity, including future people).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2604" data-end="2689">
<p data-start="2607" data-end="2689">The designers independently estimate <strong data-start="2644" data-end="2655">HAIP_RA</strong> (robust-alignment preferences).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2690" data-end="2744">
<p data-start="2693" data-end="2744">BigMomâ€™s estimate must fully encapsulate HAIP_RA.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="2746" data-end="2761">This assumes:</p>
<ul data-start="2762" data-end="3412">
<li data-start="2762" data-end="2984">
<p data-start="2764" data-end="2984">That there exists a well-defined, coherent â€œidealized preference setâ€ for humanity, when in reality philosophical traditions (utilitarianism, contractualism, pluralism, value incommensurability, etc.) all dispute this.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2985" data-end="3178">
<p data-start="2987" data-end="3178">That BigMom can extrapolate not only from current humans, but from <em data-start="3054" data-end="3062">future</em> humans, under conditions of perfect rationality and freedom â€” an impossible counterfactual to ground empirically.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3179" data-end="3412">
<p data-start="3181" data-end="3412">That human designers themselves can somehow construct an independent gold standard HAIP_RA against which to judge BigMomâ€™s version. But this is the very alignment problem: <em data-start="3353" data-end="3409">we donâ€™t know how to specify what safe alignment means</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3414" data-end="3443">The paper effectively says:</p>
<blockquote data-start="3444" data-end="3582">
<p data-start="3446" data-end="3582"><em data-start="3446" data-end="3580">We will know BigMom is safe when it can perfectly solve the hardest unsolved problem in philosophy, political theory, and AI safety.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="3584" data-end="3662">But thatâ€™s not a validation method â€” itâ€™s a restatement of the open problem.</p>
<hr data-start="3664" data-end="3667">
<h2 data-start="3669" data-end="3725">3. Meta-Handwaving: Treating Intractable as Trivial</h2>
<p data-start="3727" data-end="3775">The deeper issue is that each OAP requirement:</p>
<ul data-start="3776" data-end="4086">
<li data-start="3776" data-end="3854">
<p data-start="3778" data-end="3854"><strong data-start="3778" data-end="3807">Defines a desirable trait</strong> (inventiveness, humility, safety-awareness).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3855" data-end="3964">
<p data-start="3857" data-end="3964"><strong data-start="3857" data-end="3891">Poses an impossibly broad test</strong> (outperform humanity, aggregate all values, understand all languages).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3965" data-end="4086">
<p data-start="3967" data-end="4086"><strong data-start="3967" data-end="4006">Assumes feasibility by construction</strong> (â€œthis is gargantuan but finiteâ€; â€œSupermath will devise a superior methodâ€).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4088" data-end="4261">Instead of grappling with the unsolved nature of these challenges, the paper frames them as checklist items â€” as though once written down, they become solvable by default.</p>
<p data-start="4263" data-end="4588">This is classic handwaving: the â€œproof of safetyâ€ consists of restating the <em data-start="4339" data-end="4345">goal</em> (BigMom must be superhumanly safe, predictive, humble) in the <em data-start="4408" data-end="4427">validation method</em> (weâ€™ll test that it is superhumanly safe, predictive, humble). The gap between aspiration and implementation is erased rhetorically, not resolved technically.</p>
<hr data-start="4590" data-end="4593">
<h2 data-start="4595" data-end="4619">4. Why This Matters</h2>
<p data-start="4621" data-end="4688">This is not just a rhetorical flaw â€” itâ€™s structurally dangerous:</p>
<ul data-start="4690" data-end="5323">
<li data-start="4690" data-end="4881">
<p data-start="4692" data-end="4881">It <strong data-start="4695" data-end="4728">creates an illusion of rigor.</strong> Readers may come away believing that the OAP is a concrete roadmap, when it is really a wish-list of properties with no operational testable criteria.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4882" data-end="5119">
<p data-start="4884" data-end="5119">It <strong data-start="4887" data-end="4931">misrepresents the state of AI alignment.</strong> The hardest unsolved problems (defining general problem-solving, meaning, and value aggregation) are not highlighted as <em data-start="5052" data-end="5059">risks</em> but passed off as <em data-start="5078" data-end="5116">straightforward validation exercises</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5120" data-end="5323">
<p data-start="5122" data-end="5323">It <strong data-start="5125" data-end="5151">shifts responsibility.</strong> By embedding the open problem of alignment into the system itself (â€œBigMom will infer HAIPâ€), the paper treats the AI as its own validator, which is circular and unsafe.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="5325" data-end="5328">
<p data-start="5330" data-end="5676"><strong data-start="5330" data-end="5343">In short:</strong> the OAP checklist is a rhetorical performance of rigor that collapses under scrutiny. It relies on undefined tests, impossible scales of measurement, and assumes away the hardest philosophical and technical problems in AI. Rather than resolving the core issues of superintelligence and alignment, it handwaves them into existence.</p>
<hr data-start="5678" data-end="5681">
<p data-start="5683" data-end="5943" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="5704" data-end="5752">tighten this into a formal academic critique</strong> (structured like a peer review report), or expand it into a <strong data-start="5813" data-end="5843">longer argumentative essay</strong> with historical parallels (e.g., Turing test, IQ testing, prior â€œgrand checklistâ€ fallacies in AI)?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.5312