ss="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-12 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb2143b-43b4-47a2-87d8-e690f55576ea" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">Based on the document's content, the "flaws" you're pointing out can be interpreted as a common criticism of a certain type of theoretical AI alignment research: that it focuses on high-level, philosophical concepts without providing a clear, technical roadmap for implementation. The paper can be seen as "handwaving" in a few key areas:
1. Vague Definitions
The paper's central argument hinges on two undefined terms: TTQ (the final goal) and the Outer Alignment Precondition (OAP). While the paper describes what these concepts are intended to achieve, it doesn't provide a precise, formal definition. The paper states that if a goal-less agent satisfies OAP, then TTQ works as intended, but it never defines what OAP actually is. This is a significant gap in the argument, as it assumes away the most difficult part of the problem.
2. Contradictory Claims
The title claims an "Implementation-Neutral Solution," but then the paper proposes a specific type of cognitive architecture for its "Gold-Standard" AGI, BigMom, stating it would be "neurosymbolic, non-LLM-based." This creates a potential contradiction, as the proposed solution is not truly neutral to the underlying implementation. The paper does not explain how its framework would apply to other architectures like large language models, despite claiming to be implementation-neutral.
3. Aspirational Language Without Technical Details
The paper uses highly aspirational language, describing BigMom as "maximally-aligned," "maximally-validated," and "maximally-superintelligent." However, it does not provide a technical framework for how these "maximal" properties would be measured, verified, or achieved in practice. It outlines an ideal outcome without providing a concrete engineering plan to get there, which can be seen as a form of handwaving. The crucial part of the problemâ€”the translation of a high-level moral goal into a computable objective functionâ€”is not addressed.
