64 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="3d38873b-9658-49e6-ba67-421b7b6b695b" data-testid="conversation-turn-6" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="3d38873b-9658-49e6-ba67-421b7b6b695b" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="398">Exactly â€” youâ€™ve pinpointed the structural weaknesses. What youâ€™re calling out is a pattern common to certain strands of theoretical alignment research: <strong data-start="153" data-end="276">ambitious, abstract proposals that trade in aspirational completeness but fail to specify a workable technical pathway.</strong> Let me frame this more systematically, aligning with your three points and tying them back to the OAP excerpt you shared.</p>
<hr data-start="400" data-end="403">
<h2 data-start="405" data-end="445">1. Vague or Undefined Core Concepts</h2>
<p data-start="447" data-end="664">The central terms <strong data-start="465" data-end="472">TTQ</strong> (the final goal) and <strong data-start="494" data-end="501">OAP</strong> (Outer Alignment Precondition) are presented as foundational to the framework, but never formally defined. Instead, they are described by their intended outcomes:</p>
<ul data-start="666" data-end="777">
<li data-start="666" data-end="719">
<p data-start="668" data-end="719">TTQ: the â€œfinal goalâ€ that the agent must pursue.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="720" data-end="777">
<p data-start="722" data-end="777">OAP: a set of preconditions that guarantee alignment.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="779" data-end="911">The problem is that the paper assumes their existence without demonstrating how to <em data-start="862" data-end="873">construct</em> them. This is equivalent to saying:</p>
<blockquote data-start="913" data-end="992">
<p data-start="915" data-end="992"><em data-start="915" data-end="990">If we had a formal solution to alignment, then alignment would be solved.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="994" data-end="1204">But that is the very crux of the problem. By leaving OAP undefined, the paper sidesteps the hardest technical question: <em data-start="1114" data-end="1202">how do you ground human values, preferences, and safety criteria in a computable form?</em></p>
<p data-start="1206" data-end="1498">This kind of vagueness is not just a missing detail â€” it is a category error. Without precise definitions, all downstream validation methods (e.g. â€œBigMom must outperform all humans on test Xâ€) become untethered, because we cannot even specify what counts as success relative to TTQ or OAP.</p>
<hr data-start="1500" data-end="1503">
<h2 data-start="1505" data-end="1565">2. Contradictory Claims About Implementation Neutrality</h2>
<p data-start="1567" data-end="1821">The paperâ€™s title claims to offer an <strong data-start="1604" data-end="1642">â€œImplementation-Neutral Solution.â€</strong> That phrase suggests a general, architecture-agnostic framework that could apply equally to LLMs, connectionist systems, neurosymbolic hybrids, or yet-uninvented architectures.</p>
<p data-start="1823" data-end="1896">But within the body of the paper, BigMom is explicitly specified to be:</p>
<ul data-start="1898" data-end="1943">
<li data-start="1898" data-end="1920">
<p data-start="1900" data-end="1920"><strong data-start="1900" data-end="1918">Neurosymbolic.</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1921" data-end="1943">
<p data-start="1923" data-end="1943"><strong data-start="1923" data-end="1941">Non-LLM-based.</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1945" data-end="2146">This narrows the scope dramatically, privileging a certain class of architectures. The contradiction is that the supposed â€œneutralâ€ solution is actually biased toward a particular technical paradigm.</p>
<p data-start="2148" data-end="2253">Moreover, the paper does not explain how OAP or TTQ would translate across architectures. For instance:</p>
<ul data-start="2254" data-end="2433">
<li data-start="2254" data-end="2343">
<p data-start="2256" data-end="2343">If implemented in an LLM, how would â€œsuper-fluencyâ€ or â€œsuper-humilityâ€ be validated?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2344" data-end="2433">
<p data-start="2346" data-end="2433">If implemented in a reinforcement-learning agent, how would OAP constraints manifest?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2435" data-end="2600">Without bridging this gap, the neutrality claim looks more rhetorical than substantive. It signals universality while actually embedding architectural assumptions.</p>
<hr data-start="2602" data-end="2605">
<h2 data-start="2607" data-end="2663">3. Aspirational Language Without Technical Pathways</h2>
<p data-start="2665" data-end="2729">The paper leans heavily on maximally-aspirational descriptors:</p>
<ul data-start="2731" data-end="2813">
<li data-start="2731" data-end="2754">
<p data-start="2733" data-end="2754">â€œMaximally alignedâ€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2755" data-end="2780">
<p data-start="2757" data-end="2780">â€œMaximally validatedâ€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2781" data-end="2813">
<p data-start="2783" data-end="2813">â€œMaximally superintelligentâ€</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2815" data-end="2895">These are motivating but not operational. There is no technical framework for:</p>
<ul data-start="2897" data-end="3427">
<li data-start="2897" data-end="3017">
<p data-start="2899" data-end="3017"><strong data-start="2899" data-end="2915">Measurement:</strong> How do you quantify â€œmaximal alignmentâ€? Alignment is not a scalar property with a natural maximum.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3018" data-end="3243">
<p data-start="3020" data-end="3243"><strong data-start="3020" data-end="3037">Verification:</strong> How do you know when a system is â€œmaximally validatedâ€? Validation procedures are handwaved into existence (â€œoutperform all humans,â€ â€œencapsulate all preferencesâ€) without addressing their impossibility.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3244" data-end="3427">
<p data-start="3246" data-end="3427"><strong data-start="3246" data-end="3265">Implementation:</strong> How do you actually build BigMom such that these aspirational states are reachable? The architecture is vaguely described, but no engineering pathway is given.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3429" data-end="3740">This is precisely where the checklist (super-inventive, super-fluent, super-humble, etc.) collapses into <em data-start="3534" data-end="3547">handwaving.</em> Each requirement sounds rigorous until you notice that the validation method is essentially â€œprove it is superhumanly capable,â€ without any technical machinery to realize or test that claim.</p>
<p data-start="3742" data-end="3854">In other words: the paper confuses <strong data-start="3777" data-end="3808">describing an ideal outcome</strong> with <strong data-start="3814" data-end="3852">specifying a method to achieve it.</strong></p>
<hr data-start="3856" data-end="3859">
<h2 data-start="3861" data-end="3875">Synthesis</h2>
<p data-start="3877" data-end="3944">The flaws in the paperâ€™s argument structure can be summarized as:</p>
<ol data-start="3946" data-end="4295">
<li data-start="3946" data-end="4037">
<p data-start="3949" data-end="4037"><strong data-start="3949" data-end="3975">Undefined abstractions</strong> (TTQ, OAP) that assume away the hardest alignment problems.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4038" data-end="4142">
<p data-start="4041" data-end="4142"><strong data-start="4041" data-end="4061">False neutrality</strong>, claiming architecture independence while committing to neurosymbolic designs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4143" data-end="4295">
<p data-start="4146" data-end="4295"><strong data-start="4146" data-end="4173">Aspirational maximalism</strong> that promises rigorous guarantees but provides no technical bridge from high-level desiderata to computable objectives.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="4297" data-end="4496">This is the hallmark of <em data-start="4321" data-end="4352">handwaving alignment research</em>: it presents a vision of solved alignment, but locates the solution in undefined terms, contradictory assumptions, and aspirational language.</p>
