icle class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="09658d27-68a3-4fa4-aad7-b3f0d7d4a49b" data-testid="conversation-turn-8" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="09658d27-68a3-4fa4-aad7-b3f0d7d4a49b" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="204">Great â€” Iâ€™ll frame this as if you were submitting a <strong data-start="52" data-end="74">peer review report</strong> to an academic venue. That way it reads like a professional refereeâ€™s critique: structured, precise, but also balanced in tone.</p>
<hr data-start="206" data-end="209">
<h1 data-start="211" data-end="233">Peer Review Report</h1>
<p data-start="235" data-end="321"><strong data-start="235" data-end="263">Manuscript under review:</strong> <em data-start="264" data-end="319">An Implementation-Neutral Solution to Outer Alignment</em></p>
<hr data-start="323" data-end="326">
<h2 data-start="328" data-end="340">Summary</h2>
<p data-start="342" data-end="852">The manuscript proposes the â€œBigMomâ€ architecture as a gold-standard AGI design, grounded in the concepts of <strong data-start="451" data-end="458">TTQ</strong> (the final goal) and <strong data-start="480" data-end="487">OAP</strong> (the Outer Alignment Precondition). It lays out a set of validation requirements (super-inventive, super-fluent, super-predictive, super-knowledgeable, super-humble, safety-conscious, well-motivated, and well-resourced), accompanied by a project timeline extending over a century. The ambition is to offer a rigorous, implementation-neutral roadmap to alignment.</p>
<p data-start="854" data-end="994">While the manuscriptâ€™s scope and ambition are commendable, several central issues undermine its claims of rigor and technical feasibility.</p>
<hr data-start="996" data-end="999">
<h2 data-start="1001" data-end="1020">Major Comments</h2>
<h3 data-start="1022" data-end="1054">1. Undefined Core Concepts</h3>
<p data-start="1055" data-end="1320">The two central abstractions, <strong data-start="1085" data-end="1092">TTQ</strong> and <strong data-start="1097" data-end="1104">OAP</strong>, are never formally defined. TTQ is described only as a â€œfinal goal,â€ and OAP as a precondition that guarantees alignment, but the paper does not specify how these constructs are to be operationalized or computed.</p>
<ul data-start="1322" data-end="1624">
<li data-start="1322" data-end="1447">
<p data-start="1324" data-end="1447">Without formal definitions, downstream claims (e.g., that a goal-less agent satisfying OAP ensures TTQ) are tautological.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1448" data-end="1624">
<p data-start="1450" data-end="1624">The argument structure effectively assumes away the hardest part of the alignment problem: how to encode human values and safety conditions in machine-understandable terms.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1626" data-end="1704">This lack of definitional clarity leaves the central thesis underdetermined.</p>
<hr data-start="1706" data-end="1709">
<h3 data-start="1711" data-end="1762">2. Contradiction on Implementation Neutrality</h3>
<p data-start="1763" data-end="1880">The title claims an <strong data-start="1783" data-end="1821">â€œImplementation-Neutral Solution.â€</strong> Yet the body of the paper specifies that BigMom must be:</p>
<ul data-start="1882" data-end="1925">
<li data-start="1882" data-end="1903">
<p data-start="1884" data-end="1903"><strong data-start="1884" data-end="1901">Neurosymbolic</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1904" data-end="1925">
<p data-start="1906" data-end="1925"><strong data-start="1906" data-end="1923">Non-LLM-based</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1927" data-end="2183">This creates a contradiction: the framework is not truly neutral if it rules out certain architectures in advance. Moreover, the paper does not explain how OAP or TTQ would apply to other paradigms such as large language models or reinforcement learners.</p>
<p data-start="2185" data-end="2254">The neutrality claim thus appears more rhetorical than substantive.</p>
<hr data-start="2256" data-end="2259">
<h3 data-start="2261" data-end="2294">3. Validation as Handwaving</h3>
<p data-start="2295" data-end="2400">The OAP checklist outlines validation requirements that, on inspection, rely on unworkable assumptions:</p>
<ul data-start="2402" data-end="3226">
<li data-start="2402" data-end="2673">
<p data-start="2404" data-end="2673"><strong data-start="2404" data-end="2426">Super-human tests:</strong> For properties like problem-solving, language comprehension, or humility, validation requires outperforming all humans on some definitive test. No such universal test exists, nor is it plausible that one could be constructed across all domains.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2674" data-end="2913">
<p data-start="2676" data-end="2913"><strong data-start="2676" data-end="2710">Population-scale benchmarking:</strong> The paper acknowledges that testing against â€œas large a subset of the population of all humans as possibleâ€ is gargantuan, but dismisses this with â€œultimately finite.â€ In practice, this is infeasible.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2914" data-end="3226">
<p data-start="2916" data-end="3226"><strong data-start="2916" data-end="2941">Safety-consciousness:</strong> The validation procedure requires BigMom to estimate humanityâ€™s maximally-fairly-aggregated idealized preferences (HAIP), and compare them to an independent estimate produced by the designers. But both of these steps presuppose that the AI alignment problem has already been solved.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3228" data-end="3362">In effect, the validation framework collapses into restating the desiderata: BigMom is aligned if it demonstrates perfect alignment.</p>
<hr data-start="3364" data-end="3367">
<h3 data-start="3369" data-end="3419">4. Aspirational but Non-Operational Language</h3>
<p data-start="3420" data-end="3575">The manuscript relies heavily on maximalist descriptors (â€œmaximally-aligned,â€ â€œmaximally-validated,â€ â€œmaximally-superintelligentâ€), but does not provide:</p>
<ul data-start="3577" data-end="3737">
<li data-start="3577" data-end="3618">
<p data-start="3579" data-end="3618"><strong data-start="3579" data-end="3590">Metrics</strong> for what â€œmaximalâ€ means.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3619" data-end="3676">
<p data-start="3621" data-end="3676"><strong data-start="3621" data-end="3645">Verification methods</strong> for establishing maximality.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3677" data-end="3737">
<p data-start="3679" data-end="3737"><strong data-start="3679" data-end="3703">Engineering pathways</strong> for achieving these properties.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3739" data-end="3804">This renders the framework aspirational rather than actionable.</p>
<hr data-start="3806" data-end="3809">
<h2 data-start="3811" data-end="3830">Minor Comments</h2>
<ol data-start="3832" data-end="4380">
<li data-start="3832" data-end="3994">
<p data-start="3835" data-end="3994"><strong data-start="3835" data-end="3861">Timescale assumptions:</strong> The proposed 100-year timeline is presented with Â±50% uncertainty but no grounding in resource estimates or technical bottlenecks.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3995" data-end="4228">
<p data-start="3998" data-end="4228"><strong data-start="3998" data-end="4022">Comparative context:</strong> The paper does not situate its approach relative to prior alignment proposals (e.g., Yudkowskyâ€™s Friendly AI, Russellâ€™s value learning, Christianoâ€™s IDA). Without this, it is difficult to assess novelty.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4229" data-end="4380">
<p data-start="4232" data-end="4380"><strong data-start="4232" data-end="4261">Terminological inflation:</strong> Terms like â€œsuper-inventiveâ€ and â€œsuper-fluentâ€ risk obscuring rather than clarifying the properties being targeted.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="4382" data-end="4385">
<h2 data-start="4387" data-end="4410">Overall Evaluation</h2>
<p data-start="4412" data-end="4551">The manuscript addresses an important and urgent topic with commendable ambition. However, it suffers from serious structural weaknesses:</p>
<ul data-start="4553" data-end="4865">
<li data-start="4553" data-end="4604">
<p data-start="4555" data-end="4604">Its core abstractions (TTQ, OAP) are undefined.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4605" data-end="4677">
<p data-start="4607" data-end="4677">Its neutrality claim is contradicted by architectural prescriptions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4678" data-end="4811">
<p data-start="4680" data-end="4811">Its validation framework relies on handwaving, recasting unsolved philosophical and technical problems as simple checklist items.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4812" data-end="4865">
<p data-start="4814" data-end="4865">Its language is aspirational but non-operational.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4867" data-end="5066">At present, the paper functions more as a vision statement than as a technical roadmap. Substantial revisions are needed to transform it into a rigorous contribution to the AI alignment literature.</p>
<hr data-start="5068" data-end="5071">
<p data-start="5073" data-end="5382" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to now <strong data-start="5098" data-end="5149">recast this into a historical-comparative essay</strong> â€” showing how the flaws here echo earlier â€œgrand alignmentâ€ attempts (e.g., Omohundroâ€™s drives, Yudkowskyâ€™s FAI, Russellâ€™s cooperative IRL) â€” so it reads less like a referee report and more like a scholarly critique for publication?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.