2.1 Intelligence, problem-solving, Artificial Intelligence (AI), solvers, and agents
The fundamental concept requiring a definition is intelligence. Given our objective (Section 1.1), we
are primarily interested in utility, thus we shall equate intelligence with problem-solving6
. For our
purposes, an Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a purposefully-engineered system that solves problems7
.
A cognitive AI (or solver) takes as input a problem statement and generates a problem solution as
output. An agentic AI (or agent) ğº takes as input a stream of percepts (its percept history) and
generates a stream of actions (its action history) as output8
in pursuance of a fixed final goal9,10,11
defining an invariant on the physical universe that ğº forever strives to achieve and then maintain12,13
.
