col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="193">Perfect â€” this section really shows the pattern I flagged earlier: <strong data-start="67" data-end="176">formal-sounding definitions that look rigorous, but which actually defer or obscure the central problems.</strong> Letâ€™s unpack it.</p>
<hr data-start="195" data-end="198">
<h2 data-start="200" data-end="242">2.1 Intelligence, Solvers, and Agents</h2>
<ul data-start="244" data-end="1273">
<li data-start="244" data-end="586">
<p data-start="246" data-end="586"><strong data-start="246" data-end="281">Intelligence â‰¡ problem-solving.</strong><br data-start="281" data-end="284">
The author reduces intelligence to problem-solving for â€œour purposes.â€ This is not unusual, but it <em data-start="385" data-end="431">narrows intelligence to utility maximization</em> without defending why this reduction is adequate. Many alternative definitions (pattern recognition, compression, prediction, adaptation) are sidelined.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="588" data-end="773">
<p data-start="590" data-end="618"><strong data-start="590" data-end="616">AI as solver vs agent.</strong></p>
<ul data-start="621" data-end="773">
<li data-start="621" data-end="684">
<p data-start="623" data-end="684">Solver: takes a <em data-start="639" data-end="658">problem statement</em> â†’ outputs a <em data-start="671" data-end="681">solution</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="687" data-end="773">
<p data-start="689" data-end="773">Agent: takes <em data-start="702" data-end="712">percepts</em> â†’ outputs <em data-start="723" data-end="732">actions</em>, in service of a <strong data-start="750" data-end="770">fixed final goal</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="775" data-end="1273">
<p data-start="777" data-end="792"><strong data-start="777" data-end="790">Key move:</strong></p>
<blockquote data-start="795" data-end="982">
<p data-start="797" data-end="982"><em data-start="797" data-end="980">â€œan agent ğºâ€¦ generates a stream of actionsâ€¦ in pursuance of a fixed final goal defining an invariant on the physical universe that ğº forever strives to achieve and then maintain.â€</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="986" data-end="1273">This is a <em data-start="996" data-end="1011">huge handwave</em>. â€œA fixed final goalâ€ is assumed to exist, and moreover to be expressible as an invariant on the universe. But this is precisely the outer alignment problem: what is that invariant? How can it be represented? Instead of tackling it, the author just labels it.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1275" data-end="1278">
<h2 data-start="1280" data-end="1319">2.2 Problem Statements (âŸ¨P, Q, RâŸ©)</h2>
<ul data-start="1321" data-end="2664">
<li data-start="1321" data-end="1632">
<p data-start="1323" data-end="1341"><strong data-start="1323" data-end="1339">Formulation:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1344" data-end="1486">
<li data-start="1344" data-end="1387">
<p data-start="1346" data-end="1387"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span></span></span></span>: defines validity of solutions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1390" data-end="1441">
<p data-start="1392" data-end="1441"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8778em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Q</span></span></span></span>: defines ordering over valid solutions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1444" data-end="1486">
<p data-start="1446" data-end="1486"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span></span></span></span>: defines resource constraints.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1490" data-end="1632">So a problem statement is:<br data-start="1516" data-end="1519">
<em data-start="1521" data-end="1630">â€œfind the <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex"> Q</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8778em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">Q</span></span></span></span>-maximal element of the valid solution set <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.22222em;">V</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.2222em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, using no more than resources <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex"> R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span></span></span></span>.â€</em></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1634" data-end="1884">
<p data-start="1636" data-end="1884"><strong data-start="1636" data-end="1658">Whatâ€™s solid here:</strong><br data-start="1658" data-end="1661">
This is a neat way to formalize problem-solving as constrained optimization. In fact, it parallels standard formulations in theoretical computer science (search problems, optimization problems, constraint satisfaction).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1886" data-end="2664">
<p data-start="1888" data-end="1909"><strong data-start="1888" data-end="1907">Whatâ€™s missing:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1912" data-end="2664">
<li data-start="1912" data-end="2225">
<p data-start="1914" data-end="2225"><strong data-start="1914" data-end="1941">Problem representation.</strong> The difficulty of formalizing real-world goals as triples âŸ¨P, Q, RâŸ© is handwaved with: <em data-start="2029" data-end="2128">â€œin principle, problem statements may be arbitrarily complex, i.e. as complex as the real world.â€</em> This sidesteps the main challenge of grounding high-level human values in computable criteria.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2228" data-end="2471">
<p data-start="2230" data-end="2471"><strong data-start="2230" data-end="2258">Final goals vs subgoals.</strong> The claim that <em data-start="2274" data-end="2361">â€œany non-fixed final goal is equivalent to some fixed final goal plus internal stateâ€</em> ignores dynamic goal evolution, meta-preferences, and reflective stability â€” all major issues in alignment.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2474" data-end="2664">
<p data-start="2476" data-end="2664"><strong data-start="2476" data-end="2497">Agent motivation.</strong> Saying an agent â€œforever strives to achieve and maintainâ€ its final goal assumes persistence of motivation and ignores the thorny problem of goal misgeneralization.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2666" data-end="2669">
<h2 data-start="2671" data-end="2692">Pattern Emerging</h2>
<p data-start="2694" data-end="2720">So far, the strategy is:</p>
<ol data-start="2721" data-end="3036">
<li data-start="2721" data-end="2821">
<p data-start="2724" data-end="2821"><strong data-start="2724" data-end="2764">Introduce formal-looking scaffolding</strong> (âŸ¨P, Q, RâŸ©, agent vs solver, final goal as invariant).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2822" data-end="2876">
<p data-start="2825" data-end="2876"><strong data-start="2825" data-end="2874">Use this to give the impression of precision.</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="2877" data-end="3036">
<p data-start="2880" data-end="3036"><strong data-start="2880" data-end="2924">Avoid tackling the hardest part directly</strong> â€” namely, how to represent â€œfinal goalsâ€ like <em data-start="2971" data-end="3014">maximise the net benefit for all humanity</em> in computable form.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="3038" data-end="3169">This is why TTQ never feels concrete: it is effectively <em data-start="3094" data-end="3166">a label attached to the assumption that a computable final goal exists</em>.</p>
<hr data-start="3171" data-end="3174">
<h2 data-start="3176" data-end="3197">Why This Matters</h2>
<p data-start="3199" data-end="3296">By defining intelligence and problem-solving in this way, the author sets up a framework where:</p>
<ul data-start="3297" data-end="3444">
<li data-start="3297" data-end="3355">
<p data-start="3299" data-end="3355"><strong data-start="3299" data-end="3353">Everything looks well-defined at the formal level.</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="3356" data-end="3444">
<p data-start="3358" data-end="3444"><strong data-start="3358" data-end="3442">But the unsolved core (outer alignment) is hidden in the definitions themselves.</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3446" data-end="3453">E.g.:</p>
<ul data-start="3454" data-end="3799">
<li data-start="3454" data-end="3556">
<p data-start="3456" data-end="3556">â€œFinal goal = invariant on the universe.â€ (But what invariant? Who defines it? How is it encoded?)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3557" data-end="3660">
<p data-start="3559" data-end="3660">â€œProblem statements can be arbitrarily complex.â€ (But complexity is the problem, not the solution.)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3661" data-end="3799">
<p data-start="3663" data-end="3799">â€œA goal-less agent can later be given TTQ.â€ (But TTQ is undefined, so this is just saying â€œalignment works if you give it alignment.â€)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3801" data-end="3804">
<p data-start="3806" data-end="4130" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to <strong data-start="3824" data-end="3870">map out how TTQ shows up in later sections</strong> (and whether it ever gets beyond this âŸ¨P, Q, RâŸ© scaffolding), or would it be more useful if I drafted a <strong data-start="3975" data-end="4000">short â€œexposure noteâ€</strong> â€” a concise 1â€“2 page critique showing how the paper gives the <em data-start="4063" data-end="4075">appearance</em> of rigor while leaving the central concepts undefined?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6