items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="215">This section on <strong data-start="16" data-end="38">agent architecture</strong> repeats the same rhetorical strategy weâ€™ve been tracing: it lays out a logical skeleton that <em data-start="132" data-end="139">looks</em> rigorous, but the â€œhard problemsâ€ are smuggled into undefined placeholders.</p>
<hr data-start="217" data-end="220">
<h2 data-start="222" data-end="249">What the Author is Doing</h2>
<ol data-start="251" data-end="905">
<li data-start="251" data-end="558">
<p data-start="254" data-end="306"><strong data-start="254" data-end="304">Decomposing an agent into two core mechanisms:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="310" data-end="558">
<li data-start="310" data-end="414">
<p data-start="312" data-end="414"><strong data-start="312" data-end="344">Continuous Learning (CL_G):</strong> builds and updates a Theory-of-the-Universe (ToU_G) from percepts.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="418" data-end="558">
<p data-start="420" data-end="558"><strong data-start="420" data-end="452">Continuous Planning (CP_G):</strong> generates plans, given ToU_G and the final goal FG_G, which in turn produce the action history AH_G.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="560" data-end="722">
<p data-start="563" data-end="581"><strong data-start="563" data-end="579">Causal flow:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="585" data-end="722">
<li data-start="585" data-end="643">
<p data-start="587" data-end="643">Physical universe PU â†’ percepts PH_G â†’ CL_G â†’ ToU_G</p>
</li>
<li data-start="647" data-end="722">
<p data-start="649" data-end="722">ToU_G + FG_G â†’ CP_G â†’ plans/actions AH_G â†’ effectors â†’ back into PU</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="724" data-end="905">
<p data-start="727" data-end="905"><strong data-start="727" data-end="749">Logical necessity:</strong> The paper asserts that any agent <em data-start="783" data-end="789">must</em> operate in a way equivalent to this structure. This is a generalization of the senseâ€“planâ€“act loop from classic AI.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="907" data-end="910">
<h2 data-start="912" data-end="944">Where the Handwaving Shows Up</h2>
<ol data-start="946" data-end="2383">
<li data-start="946" data-end="1343">
<p data-start="949" data-end="994"><strong data-start="949" data-end="992">Final Goal (FG_G) as Input to Planning</strong></p>
<ul data-start="998" data-end="1343">
<li data-start="998" data-end="1101">
<p data-start="1000" data-end="1101">The paper assumes every agent has a <em data-start="1036" data-end="1058">final goal invariant</em> that defines what it strives to achieve.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1105" data-end="1211">
<p data-start="1107" data-end="1211">But this is the exact open problem: <strong data-start="1143" data-end="1209">what is FG_G, how is it specified, and how is it represented?</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1215" data-end="1343">
<p data-start="1217" data-end="1343">Saying â€œit must exist or else CP_G wouldnâ€™t know what to doâ€ is circular â€” it assumes away the problem of goal specification.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1345" data-end="1749">
<p data-start="1348" data-end="1385"><strong data-start="1348" data-end="1383">Theory-of-the-Universe (ToU_G)</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1389" data-end="1749">
<li data-start="1389" data-end="1457">
<p data-start="1391" data-end="1457">CL_G is said to maintain a ToU consistent with percept history.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1461" data-end="1649">
<p data-start="1463" data-end="1649">But how is this constructed? In practice, <em data-start="1505" data-end="1528">model-building itself</em> is one of the hardest unsolved problems (cf. world models in AI research, epistemic uncertainty, simulation fidelity).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1653" data-end="1749">
<p data-start="1655" data-end="1749">Here, itâ€™s treated as a black box: â€œCL_G just maintains a theory consistent with percepts.â€</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1751" data-end="2078">
<p data-start="1754" data-end="1784"><strong data-start="1754" data-end="1782">Degenerate possibilities</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1788" data-end="2078">
<li data-start="1788" data-end="1922">
<p data-start="1790" data-end="1922">The author notes that CP_G could randomly plan, or CL_G could maintain a fixed theory â€” but dismisses these as degenerate cases.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1926" data-end="2078">
<p data-start="1928" data-end="2078">This sidesteps the fact that <strong data-start="1957" data-end="2036">most realistic architectures today are far closer to these degenerate cases</strong> than to the imagined Gold-Standard AGI.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="2080" data-end="2383">
<p data-start="2083" data-end="2105"><strong data-start="2083" data-end="2103">Goal-less agents</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2109" data-end="2383">
<li data-start="2109" data-end="2383">
<p data-start="2111" data-end="2383">The paper claims a goal-less agent wonâ€™t have purposeful effects on the universe. But this ignores that any system with perception and action has <em data-start="2257" data-end="2274">implicit drives</em> (minimization of free energy, predictive stability, etc.). â€œGoal-lessâ€ is not actually a neutral category.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="2385" data-end="2388">
<h2 data-start="2390" data-end="2411">The Bigger Picture</h2>
<p data-start="2413" data-end="2466">This â€œlogical architectureâ€ is a <strong data-start="2446" data-end="2463">cartoon model</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="2467" data-end="2898">
<li data-start="2467" data-end="2557">
<p data-start="2469" data-end="2557">It resembles Newell &amp; Simonâ€™s senseâ€“planâ€“act loop, dressed up in more formal notation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2558" data-end="2898">
<p data-start="2560" data-end="2648">But it doesnâ€™t solve (or even confront) the <strong data-start="2604" data-end="2645">three hardest challenges of alignment</strong>:</p>
<ol data-start="2651" data-end="2898">
<li data-start="2651" data-end="2697">
<p data-start="2654" data-end="2697"><strong data-start="2654" data-end="2695">What is the final goal (FG_G / TTQ)?</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="2700" data-end="2783">
<p data-start="2703" data-end="2783"><strong data-start="2703" data-end="2781">How can an agentâ€™s ToU faithfully capture reality, given epistemic limits?</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="2786" data-end="2898">
<p data-start="2789" data-end="2898"><strong data-start="2789" data-end="2896">How do we ensure plans generated under imperfect ToU donâ€™t catastrophically misalign with human values?</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2900" data-end="3119">Instead, it <strong data-start="2912" data-end="2942">assumes these are possible</strong>, then labels the assumed solutions with symbols (FG_G, CL_G, CP_G, ToU_G). The notation gives a false impression of rigor, while the core alignment problems are deferred.</p>
<hr data-start="3121" data-end="3124">
<h2 data-start="3126" data-end="3163">Why This Matters for Your Critique</h2>
<p data-start="3165" data-end="3240">The architecture section is another example of â€œalignment by definitionâ€:</p>
<ul data-start="3241" data-end="3394">
<li data-start="3241" data-end="3281">
<p data-start="3243" data-end="3281">FG_G exists (but is never defined).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3282" data-end="3325">
<p data-start="3284" data-end="3325">ToU_G exists (but is never explained).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3326" data-end="3394">
<p data-start="3328" data-end="3394">CP_G produces aligned plans (because it uses FG_G and ToU_G).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3396" data-end="3507">Thus, the system is â€œproven safeâ€ simply by stipulating that its components do the things we want them to do.</p>
<p data-start="3509" data-end="3562">Itâ€™s a <strong data-start="3516" data-end="3559">logical closure, not a technical design</strong>.</p>
